            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more, and the set is complete."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: belonging to, or the place for",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish -ario is English -ary."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin -ārius meant belonging to — and then, by an easy step, the place where that thing is kept."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "diccionario — the place the words are kept."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which is exactly what an English dictionary is, and neither language ever said so out loud."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Spanish",
                "English"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 5,
              "utterances": [
                "necesario means necessary.",
                "ordinario means ordinary.",
                "primario means primary.",
                "diccionario means dictionary.",
                "aniversario means anniversary."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: it does two jobs",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Unlike the other seven, -ario makes both nouns and adjectives, and the sentence tells you which:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "un diccionario, a dictionary — a thing.",
                "es necesario, it is necessary — a quality."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "When it is an adjective it ends in -o, so it shifts the way you already expect: necesario / necesaria. When it is a noun it is masculine, and takes el or un."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You do not have to decide in advance. The sentence decides."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
